  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1038 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nHigh pressure is centered over the S.Rockies and amplified\nacross the Canadian Prairies to the Arctic...being squeezed\nbetween deeper upstream and downstream troughs. There is also a\ntropical system in the SE Pacific that will be feeding moisture\ninto the ring of fire around the high. This keeps the highest\nPWAT upstream of us to end the week then feed the better forcing\nas we head into the weekend as the upstream trough begins to\nbreak down the Rockies ridge. Dynamic trop fields show a weak\nwave moving through the CWA overnight which will at least keep\nsome clouds if not very isolated showers around through sunrise.\nThis leaves us under weak forcing again for tomorrow afternoon\nand mainly terrain forced convection expected similar to today.\nDrier low layers will favor gusty outflow winds more than heavy\nrainfall but the right storm over a recent burn scar could still\ncause some issues. Friday will again be similar however the\nwinds look be picking up a bit as the upstream trough moves\nfarther inland and begins to impinge upon the ridge and tighten\nthe SW gradient aloft. HDWI shows a weak signal over parts of\neastern Utah into NW Colorado leading to localized areas of\ncritical fire weather. Temperatures during this time remain\nabove normal. \n\nThe northeast Pacific remains an active upstream impact to the \npattern shift Saturday and beyond. A deeper pool of cooler, \nwetter air arrives Saturday. This, dare I say, looks more fall- \nlike given the erosion of the monsoonal high pressure pattern. \nI'm sure we still have some warm days ahead of us, but this next\nweek looks cooler and wetter. Tropical storm activity in the \neastern Pacific could play into local moisture supplies later \nnext week. This plays into September climatology and could \nsupply some needed moisture to the West Slope...fingers crossed.",
